    "url": "https://www.zoebnomi.com/crosssource/",
    "text": "Items\n150\nOpinions\n45\nTopics\n15\nRater kappa\n0.98 / 0.84\nEval harnesses\n→ CrossSource\nmirror-eval\nscreener-eval\ngithub.com/zoeb-nomi/crosssource CrossSource: measuring whether a RAG system can be trusted to cite the law\nStrict citation prompting lifted citation precision from 0.981 to 0.994 on 25 questions; recall stayed at 0.760 because retrieval, not the model, was the limit. CrossSource is the open harness behind that number: when a legal RAG system cites a court opinion, does the opinion support the claim?\nFig. 1 Which number moved. v0.1, n = 25. Hollow: baseline prompt. Filled: strict prompt.\nCitation precision\n0.70 1.00\n0.981 → 0.994\nCitation recall\n0.70 1.00\n0.760 → 0.760\nCrossSource v0.1, Table 2 below. Axis starts at 0.70.\n§1 Why this exists\nIn Mata v. Avianca , lawyers were sanctioned for filing a brief full of citations an AI invented. Citation hallucination is the failure legal and tax AI most has to stop before a practitioner relies on an answer. It is the same failure I work on in production. CrossSource is the public, reproducible version of that work: a dataset where every item traces to an exact passage in a public opinion, a written labelling protocol, and an evaluation pipeline anyone can run.\n§2 Method\nv0.3 dataset\n150 items: 75 qa_citation , 75 citation_check: over 45 public US opinions from CourtListener (27 federal circuit, 18 state supreme court, 2018 or later), across 15 legal topics.\nProvenance\nEvery item carries its opinion ID, URL, the exact passage and the cited authority.\nLabelling\nA written protocol, LABELING_PROTOCOL.md v1.1. Items are model-drafted and model-checked; no human labelled every item.\nAgreement\nTwo blind, independent model raters: Cohen's kappa 0.98 on citation_check, 0.84 on qa_citation.\nAdjudication\nEvery disagreement plus a 10% audit sample, 30 items in all, adjudicated by me with a third independent model rater. 27 of 30 matched the recommended label.\nv0.1 corpus\n22 public-domain US court opinions (16 Supreme Court, 2 federal circuit, 4 state), sourced from Harvard Law School's Caselaw Access Project, chunked to ~350 words.\nv0.1 retrieval\nBM25, top-5.\nv0.1 generation\nClaude with mandatory inline citations in doc_id:chunk_id format.\nv0.1 golden set\n25 human-authored questions with verified ground-truth supporting chunks.\nv0.1 configurations\nbaseline (citation formatting only) vs strict (explicit citation-discipline rules in the prompt).\nv0.1 scoring\nclaim-level citation precision and recall, faithfulness, and answer relevance: scored by an LLM judge, validated by hand (below).\n§3 Findings\nTable 1. v0.3 sanity run on a 30-item sample (not a full run)\nTable 1. v0.3 sanity run on a 30-item sample (not a full run) Model Citation precision Citation recall citation_check accuracy\nclaude-sonnet-5-5 0.919 0.718 1.00\ngpt-5-mini 0.763 0.718 0.93\nscroll &rarr;\nFig. 1 Which layer owns the failure Replay\nBaseline prompt 9 failures\nStrict prompt 7 failures\nwrong passage, tied to the prompt missing authority, traces to retrieval absent under the strict prompt\nCitation precision\n0.981 → 0.994\nCitation recall\n0.760 → 0.760\nThe strict prompt had 2 fewer wrong-passage failures than baseline (3 → 1). The 6 missing-authority failures were the same in both configurations and trace to retrieval.\nSource: Tables 2 and 3 below (v0.1, n = 25). Wrong-passage failures fell under the strict prompt; missing-authority failures trace to retrieval.\nTable 2. v0.1 (n = 25): baseline vs. strict\nTable 2. v0.1 (n = 25): baseline vs. strict Dimension Baseline Strict\nCitation precision 0.981 0.994\nCitation recall 0.760 0.760\nFaithfulness 1.000 1.000\nAnswer relevance 0.980 0.960\nscroll &rarr;\nTable 3. v0.1 (n = 25): failure taxonomy by configuration\nTable 3. v0.1 (n = 25): failure taxonomy by configuration Error type Baseline Strict\nRight document, wrong passage 3 1\nMissing authority 6 6\nWrong document 0 0\nUnsupported claim 0 0\nscroll &rarr;\nThe v0.1 finding that still stands (n = 25): citation-discipline prompting buys precision: wrong-passage citations drop 3 → 1, precision rises 0.981 → 0.994. It cannot buy recall: 0.760 in both configurations, capped by retrieval, because every missing-authority failure traces to retrieval, not generation. Knowing which layer owns the failure is the entire point of separating the metrics.\n§4 The judge caught a bug, because I checked the judge\nv0.1 validation pass: stratified blind sample, hand-graded.\nIn v0.1, I hand-graded a blind, stratified sample of the judge's citation-precision verdicts:\n100% agreement (15/15).\nThat validation pass also surfaced a genuine harness defect: consecutive citations produced punctuation-only claim spans that the judge initially mislabeled as failures. Fixing it changed the metrics.\nFig. 2 One answer through the harness\nGo to step 1 : The answer\nGo to step 2 : Claim spans\nGo to step 3 : The bug\nGo to step 4 : The judge scores it\nGo to step 5 : The blind check\nPlay Back Next 0:00 Speed 0.5× 1× 1.5× 2×\nTranscript\nThe answer. The model answers a legal question and cites its sources inline.\nClaim spans. The harness cuts the answer into claim spans. The judge will check each one against the passage it cites.\nThe bug. Where two citations sit side by side, a span is left holding nothing but punctuation, a fragment like \".\" that is not a claim.\nThe judge scores it. The judge dutifully scores that span as a citation failure. A harness bug now looks like a model failure.\nThe blind check. I hand-graded a blind, stratified sample of the judge's verdicts: 15 of 15 agreed. Checking the judge is what exposed the bug.\nSource: this section and the judge essay. The spans drawn are placeholders, not real answer text. Read the essay →\nThat is the lesson worth paying for: an unvalidated eval reports wrong numbers with full confidence, precisely the failure mode the eval exists to catch.\n§5 What this demonstrates\nThe same discipline I use on production systems and on anything I build:\nInstrument the pipeline\nKeep provenance for every item, so any score traces back to a passage\nBuild a failure taxonomy instead of a single score\nCheck the judge against people before trusting it\nSeparate precision from recall, so you know whether to fix the prompt or the retrieval\n§6 Release (v0.3)\nReleased 20 Oct 2026\nv0.3 release notes\nDOI 10.5281/zenodo.23493797 (v0.3)\nDOI 10.5281/zenodo.21969577 (all versions)\n§7 Related writing\nThe judge caught a bug I didn't , on validating the LLM judge behind these numbers.\nmirror-eval , the same method pointed at what AI search engines say about me.\nscreener-eval , the same counting discipline pointed at LLM résumé screeners, no judge at all.\nAI PRODUCT MANAGER · AGENTS, WORKFLOWS AND EVALS\nRead the code, run the harness\nView the repo → Book a call"
